/*!
    \page tutorial-install-and-run.html
    \title Tutorial 2: Install Felgo and Run the Game
    \previouspage {Tutorial 1: What We Build}
    \nextpage {Tutorial 3: The Felgo Project}

    This chapter takes you from nothing to a running game.

    \section1 1. Install Felgo

    \list 1
        \li Go to \l {https://felgo.com/download}{felgo.com/download} and create a free account.
        \li Download and start the Felgo installer.
        \li Select Felgo 4 with Qt 6.8.3. On Windows pick the \b {MinGW 64-bit} desktop kit.
        \li Finish the installation. It includes Qt Creator, CMake, Ninja and the compiler.
    \endlist

    \section1 2. Get the code

    \badcode
    git clone https://github.com/comm0/creature_wars.git
    \endcode

    \section1 3. Open the project

    \list 1
        \li Start Qt Creator from the Felgo installation.
        \li \uicontrol {File > Open File or Project} and select \c CMakeLists.txt.
        \li Select the \b {Felgo Desktop Qt 6.8.3 MinGW 64-bit} kit and click
            \uicontrol {Configure Project}.
    \endlist

    The first configure downloads nlohmann/json from GitHub, so you need internet.

    \section1 4. Build and run

    Select the \c appCreatureWars target and press \uicontrol Run (Ctrl+R). You get the start
    screen. Pick a race and press \uicontrol Start.

    In Debug builds a second window, \e {Creature Wars Debug}, shows log messages and the
    game tick time. That is normal.

    \note Android is on the todo list. It is not tested yet, so this tutorial covers the
    desktop build only.

    \section1 5. Run the unit tests (optional)

    The core has its own tests that need no Felgo:

    \badcode
    cmake -S . -B build/core -DCREATURE_WARS_BUILD_APP=OFF -DBUILD_TESTING=ON
    cmake --build build/core
    ctest --test-dir build/core
    \endcode
*/
